13. Performance principles already agreed
Useful techniques:

Distinguish hosting cold starts, fresh extraction, and result-cache hits.

Reuse initialized adapter code.

Deduplicate identical in-flight requests.

Preserve cancellation semantics: one disconnected client must not cancel another client’s shared request.

Keep cache TTLs conservative around signed URLs and IP/session restrictions.

Match concurrency to actual server capacity.

Bound slow providers rather than always waiting indefinitely.

Keep browser-backed extraction isolated.

Do not claim link extraction proves playback.

Do not claim caching establishes faster fresh extraction.

Future shared-task tests should cover:

Same-title concurrent requests execute once.

Different titles do not collapse together.

Cancellation of one subscriber leaves the other intact.

Last-subscriber cancellation stops unnecessary work.

Failure does not poison future retries.

Deadline and cache expiry behavior.

Provider-specific headers and quality settings remain correct.

Do not let optimization become an endless detour that prevents adding sources and user-visible features.

